g reduction in probability."}, {"t": 5, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient’s recent rapid weight loss and chronic alcohol consumption contribute to the formation of radio-opaque stones in the common bile duct, making choledocholithiasis more likely?", "summary": "Chronic alcohol use increases biliary sludge risk (moderate_for for choledocholithiasis), but rapid weight loss and alcohol alone do not strongly support radio-opaque stones."}, {"t": 5, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient’s chronic alcohol use and recent rapid weight loss strongly support biliary sludge embolism as the cause of her acute RUQ pain and shock?", "summary": "Chronic alcohol use provides weak support for biliary sludge embolism, but the absence of hepatomegaly/ascites does not rule it out."}, {"t": 5, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of Murphy’s sign and jaundice in a patient with right upper quadrant pain and hypotension contradict acute cholecystitis as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "The absence of Murphy’s sign and jaundice moderately contradicts acute cholecystitis as the primary diagnosis."}, {"t": 5, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of documented hepatomegaly or ascites in a patient with suspected hepatic vascular pathology rule out biliary sludge embolism as a dangerous diagnosis?", "summary": "The absence of hepatomegaly/ascites does not rule out biliary sludge embolism, as this finding has minimal diagnostic impact."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.423], ["B2.2", 0.392], ["B4", 0.103]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.554], ["B1", 0.261], ["B4", 0.185]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.468], ["B1", 0.269], ["B4", 0.262]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Patient exhibits hypotension (80/40 mmHg) with tachycardia (110 bpm) and severe localized right upper quadrant pain, fulfilling criteria for shock and perfusion compromise. Gynecological hemorrhage (e.g., ruptured ovarian 